WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:04.230
Mina is waiting for a ride
outside a busy station.

2
00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:06.900
Her phone puts her
on the other side of the street.

3
00:00:07.290 --> 00:00:10.611
So why does your location go wrong
among tall buildings?

4
00:00:10.901 --> 00:00:12.891
Your phone listens to satellites.

5
00:00:12.891 --> 00:00:17.504
GPS has at least 24,
about 20,000 kilometres up.

6
00:00:17.594 --> 00:00:21.864
Each one sends a radio message:
where it is, and the precise time.

7
00:00:21.954 --> 00:00:23.544
It travels at the speed of light,

8
00:00:23.544 --> 00:00:27.526
so the delay tells the phone
how far away that satellite is.

9
00:00:27.616 --> 00:00:29.986
With distances to at least four satellites,

10
00:00:29.986 --> 00:00:32.181
the phone can work out where it is.

11
00:00:32.521 --> 00:00:35.805
But this assumes each signal
flies straight to the phone.

12
00:00:35.995 --> 00:00:39.275
On Mina’s street, tall buildings
hide much of the sky,

13
00:00:39.275 --> 00:00:42.545
and a signal can bounce
off a glass wall to reach her.

14
00:00:42.635 --> 00:00:44.275
The bounce makes the trip longer,

15
00:00:44.275 --> 00:00:47.325
so that satellite seems
farther away than it is.

16
00:00:47.415 --> 00:00:50.175
A few wrong distances,
and the dot slides,

17
00:00:50.175 --> 00:00:52.365
often to the wrong side of the street.

18
00:00:52.705 --> 00:00:55.255
It’s like a friend calling
from around a corner:

19
00:00:55.255 --> 00:00:59.246
their voice bounces off a wall
and takes the long way to you.

20
00:00:59.336 --> 00:01:01.086
That’s where the comparison stops.

21
00:01:01.086 --> 00:01:02.746
You might notice an echo.

22
00:01:02.746 --> 00:01:04.820
A phone that can’t spot the bounce

23
00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:07.293
counts the extra delay as distance.

24
00:01:07.383 --> 00:01:10.583
At the speed of light,
one ten-millionth of a second

25
00:01:10.583 --> 00:01:11.910
is about 30 metres.

26
00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:15.370
Phones also use Wi-Fi
and mobile networks.

27
00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:19.440
But tall buildings can get between you
and the mobile towers too.

28
00:01:19.630 --> 00:01:21.110
So when the app is unsure,

29
00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:23.970
it may draw a light blue circle
around the dot.

30
00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:25.950
It’s an estimate, not a promise.

31
00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:29.340
A smaller circle means
a more confident estimate.

32
00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:31.430
Mina sees a big circle.

33
00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:34.100
She walks along the pavement
to the open corner,

34
00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:36.980
where more sky may help,
sets the pin herself,

35
00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:39.620
and tells the driver:
by the pharmacy.

36
00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:41.670
This is the simple version.

37
00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:45.430
Some phones can correct for bounces,
but not every time.

38
00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:48.270
The atmosphere and the receiver
matter too.

39
00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:51.840
When the circle is big,
trust a landmark, not the dot.
